Anaphylactoid purpura
Rheumatology & Immunology
Palpable purpura, Colicky abdominal pain, IgA tissue deposition

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-001
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of spreading palpable purpura, migrating arthralgias, and severe colicky abdominal pain requiring immediate evaluation to rule out acute surgical abdomen and initiate timely intervention to prevent renal or systemic deterioration.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and caregiver
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated rapid evolution of rash, severe abdominal pain, and inability to ambulate due to joint swelling. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly required to differentiate from acute meningococcemia, exclude intussusception, and formulate a safe management plan, as the presentation could not be safely delayed until the next available in-hours consultation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My legs are covered in these raised purple spots, my knees ache so much I can barely stand, and my stomach keeps cramping up."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports an evolving constellation of symptoms starting 48 hours ago with an initial mild upper respiratory tract infection. Over the past 24 hours, progressive non-blanching palpable purpura emerged predominantly distributed on the buttocks and lower extremities. Concurrently, migratory arthralgias typically affecting the knees and ankles developed, leading to significant periarticular swelling and restricted mobility. In the last 12 hours, episodic colicky abdominal pain commenced, compounded by nausea, prompting the after-hours call. Denies frank gastrointestinal bleeding or melena at this stage. Noted to have intermittent subcutaneous edema of the hands and feet.
Functional Status: Housebound, ambulating with difficulty due to knee arthralgia.
Social/Safety Concerns: Caregiver present but anxious regarding the rapidly spreading rash.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 37.6 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, visibly uncomfortable during waves of abdominal cramping. Mild diaphoresis present.
Physical Exam: Dermatological: Widespread, non-blanching palpable purpura distributed primarily on the gravity-dependent lower extremities and buttocks. Musculoskeletal: Periarticular swelling and marked tenderness over bilateral knee and ankle joints; range of motion limited by pain. Abdomen: Soft, non-distended; diffuse tenderness without guarding, rigidity, or palpable masses to suggest intussusception. Renal: Urine dipstick positive for microscopic hematuria (+1) and trace proteinuria indicating early renal involvement. Diagnostic Tetrad integration highlights palpable purpura, colicky abdominal pain, and clinical suspicion pending formal IgA tissue deposition confirmation. Normal platelet counts directly excluding thrombocytopenia.
Environmental Observations: Clean living space, well-lit, no obvious tripping hazards, heating functional.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications; paracetamol taken prior to visit with minimal effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Anaphylactoid purpura - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Meningococcemia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of high fever, no profound hemodynamic instability, and rash is dependent and predominantly palpable rather than purely petechial/purpuric with toxic appearance.

Immune thrombocytopenic purpura - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of palpable purpura (rather than flat petechiae), pronounced arthralgias, abdominal involvement, and assumed normal platelet count.

Acute appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of focal right lower quadrant peritonitis; pain is diffuse and colicky, accompanied by defining systemic dermatological and articular signs.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, progressive triad of spreading palpable purpura, debilitating migratory arthralgia, and significant colicky abdominal pain. The combination of systemic vasculitic signs and abdominal symptomatology necessitated an urgent, after-hours physical examination to rule out immediate surgical emergencies, including intussusception or bowel ischemia, and acute severe sepsis. Delaying this assessment until the next standard in-hours period posed an unacceptable risk of profound gastrointestinal, renal, or systemic deterioration. Immediate bedside evaluation confirmed an evolving but hemodynamically stable presentation of Anaphylactoid purpura, satisfying MBS 585-600 criteria for urgent after-hours care.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Provide adequate analgesia utilizing NSAIDs (if no major GI bleed risk or severe renal impairment) or paracetamol. Recommend baseline laboratory evaluation including full blood count, urea/electrolytes, creatinine, and formal urinalysis within 24 hours to quantify hematuria/proteinuria.
Positive Behavioral Change: Fluid intake reinforcement: Drink 250ml of water every two hours while awake; succeeding in maintaining hydration will demonstrably reduce subjective fatigue and support renal perfusion. Rest protocol: Keep legs elevated above the level of the pelvis when seated; achieving this consistently will mechanically decrease the dependent purpuric rash and periarticular swelling, positively reinforcing the behavior with visible symptom reduction.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None at present.
DME/Safety (Optional): None at present.
Follow-up: Routine GP review required within 24-48 hours for laboratory results and renal function monitoring.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present immediately to the Emergency Department or call 000 if experiencing severe, unremitting abdominal pain, intractable vomiting, frank blood in stool or urine, sudden oliguria, or any signs of hemodynamic instability such as dizziness or confusion.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending formal laboratory investigations; systemic conditions are dynamic and subject to change.
Clinical Course: Expected natural course is typically self-limiting over several weeks, but close monitoring is required as factors favoring resolution depend on preventing renal and gastrointestinal complications.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency medical attention if abdominal pain changes from cramping to constant severe agony, if you vomit blood or pass black/bloody stools, or if you stop passing urine over a 6-hour period.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Paracetamol and rest are initiated to manage joint pain and reduce the mechanical stress driving the dependent purpura, while avoiding medications that could irritate the stomach or worsen potential kidney issues until blood tests are completed.
Actionable Steps: Elevate the legs whenever resting to visibly reduce swelling and new spot formation. Maintain strict hydration targets to support kidney function.

